                  "response": {
                    "result": "Here is a list of highly rated romance movies, encompassing various styles and eras of the genre:\n\n*   **Titanic (1997)**: Directed by James Cameron and starring Leonardo DiCaprio and Kate Winslet, this epic romance tells the story of a forbidden love amidst the tragic maiden voyage of the RMS Titanic. The film earned 11 Oscars and remains one of the highest-grossing films of all time.\n*   **Casablanca (1942)**: This iconic Golden Age Hollywood film stars Humphrey Bogart and Ingrid Bergman as estranged lovers who are reunited in war-torn Casablanca. It is often considered the ultimate romance film, renowned for its timeless dialogue and themes of love and sacrifice.\n*   **The Notebook (2004)**: Starring Ryan Gosling and Rachel McAdams, this popular film is a classic tale of enduring love, following a couple's relationship through various stages of their lives.\n*   **Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind (2004)**: Featuring Jim Carrey and Kate Winslet, this unique and critically acclaimed movie explores a couple's decision to erase each other from their memories after a painful breakup. Its experimental storytelling earned it an Academy Award for Best Original Screenplay.\n*   **Dirty Dancing (1987)**: This classic stars Patrick Swayze and Jennifer Grey in a story of a young woman who falls in love with her dance instructor at a summer resort. It features iconic choreography and memorable lines.\n*   **Amélie (2001)**: A whimsical French romantic comedy, *Amélie* follows the titular character as she secretly orchestrates the lives of those around her, ultimately finding her own love. The film is celebrated for its unique visual style and heartwarming narrative.\n*   **Brokeback Mountain (2005)**: Directed by Ang Lee and starring Jake Gyllenhaal and Heath Ledger, this film was groundbreaking as one of the first mainstream movies to portray a same-sex love story. It garnered three Oscars, including Best Director.\n*   **Portrait of a Lady on Fire (2019)**: Set in 18th-century France, this acclaimed film tells the passionate story of a painter and her subject, who fall in love while the portrait is being created. It received awards at the Cannes Film Festival.\n*   **The Before Trilogy (Before Sunrise, 1995; Before Sunset, 2004; Before Midnight, 2013)**: This series of films, directed by Richard Linklater and starring Ethan Hawke and Julie Delpy, chronicles the evolving relationship of Jesse and Celine over nearly two decades, primarily through their conversations.\n*   **La La Land (2016)**: Starring Emma Stone and Ryan Gosling, this modern musical romance depicts the aspirations and relationship of a jazz musician and an aspiring actress in Los Angeles.\n*   **When Harry Met Sally (1989)**: A quintessential romantic comedy, this film explores the enduring question of whether men and women can truly be just friends.\n*   **In the Mood for Love (2000)**: This visually stunning film from Hong Kong, directed by Wong Kar-wai, explores themes of longing and unfulfilled desire between two neighbors in 1960s Hong Kong."
